DEEP[1]=("Chapter 4 is named Jñāna-Karma-Sannyāsa Yoga, the yoga of knowledge and the renunciation of action. Vivasvān, the Sun god, is the father of Vaivasvata Manu, the Manu of our present age. Ikṣvāku, Manu’s son, founded the solar dynasty (Sūrya-vaṃśa) that later produced Rāma.",
 "<i>Imaṃ vivasvate yogaṃ proktavān aham avyayam</i>, “this imperishable yoga I taught to Vivasvān”. Commentators note several points. The yoga is called <i>avyaya</i>, imperishable, because its fruit, liberation, does not decay. The line of transmission passes through kings, to show that karma yoga suits rulers and householders, not only monks. Śaṅkara says the Lord teaches it to kṣatriyas so that they can protect the world with strength rooted in wisdom. Rāmānuja calls this the Lord revealing his eternal teaching role. Some modern readers link the Sun to the light of knowledge: the teaching begins with light itself.",
 "The best wisdom is often old and has been tested by many generations. Look for teachings that have lasted.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Evaṃ paramparā-prāptam</i>, “thus received by succession”. <i>Imaṃ rājarṣayo viduḥ</i>, “the royal sages knew it”. <i>Sa kāleneha mahatā</i>, “by long passage of time here”. <i>Yogo naṣṭaḥ parantapa</i>, “the yoga was lost, scorcher of foes”.",
 "<i>Rājarṣi</i>, royal sage, is a ruler who is also a seer, like Janaka (3.20). Commentators explain why the teaching was lost. Śaṅkara says it fell into the hands of those weak in self-control, who could not keep it. Rāmānuja says people lost the ability to understand it. Madhva says the knowledge was hidden in times of decline. Whatever the reason, the verse admits something important: even true teaching can fade. That is why the Lord renews it (4.7–8). Indian traditions place great weight on <i>paramparā</i>, the living chain of teachers, for this reason.",
 "If you have learned something valuable, teach it. A chain is only as strong as each link.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Sa evāyaṃ mayā te’dya</i>, “this same yoga, by me, to you today”. <i>Yogaḥ proktaḥ purātanaḥ</i>, “the ancient yoga is taught”. <i>Bhakto’si me sakhā ceti</i>, “for you are my devotee and friend”. <i>Rahasyaṃ hy etad uttamam</i>, “this is the highest secret”.",
 "Commentators find this one of the Gītā’s tenderest verses. Arjuna receives the teaching not because he is learned or a renunciant, but because he is <i>bhakta</i>, devoted, and <i>sakhā</i>, a friend. Rāmānuja’s school treasures <i>sakhā</i>: the Lord’s friendship is his grace in human form. Śaṅkara says “secret” means it is not for everyone, only for those ready. In 18.64, near the end, Kṛṣṇa will repeat: “you are dearly loved by me, so I tell you this for your good”. The Gītā is framed as a conversation between friends.",
 "We open up most to those we trust. If you want to learn deeply, build relationships of trust.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Aparaṃ bhavato janma</i>, “your birth was later”. <i>Paraṃ janma vivasvataḥ</i>, “Vivasvān’s birth earlier”. <i>Katham etad vijānīyām</i>, “how am I to understand this?”. <i>Tvam ādau proktavān iti</i>, “that you taught it in the beginning”.",
 "This is Arjuna’s fourth question. Commentators note it is a sincere puzzle: Arjuna has known Kṛṣṇa all his life as a cousin, born to Vasudeva and Devakī. Some, like Śaṅkara, suggest Arjuna asks to remove doubts others might have. Rāmānuja says Arjuna knows Kṛṣṇa is divine but wants to understand how the eternal Lord takes birth. The question is the doorway to one of the most important teachings in Hinduism: the avatāra. Good questions, the Gītā shows, unlock deep answers.",
 "If something does not add up, say so. Honest puzzlement leads to understanding.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Bahūni me vyatītāni</i>, “many of mine have passed”. <i>Janmāni tava cārjuna</i>, “births, and yours too, Arjuna”. <i>Tāny ahaṃ veda sarvāṇi</i>, “I know them all”. <i>Na tvaṃ vettha parantapa</i>, “you do not know them”.",
 "The verse confirms two kinds of birth: Arjuna’s, driven by karma, and Kṛṣṇa’s, by choice. Commentators note that Arjuna and Kṛṣṇa are often identified with the ancient sages Nara and Nārāyaṇa, who practised austerity together at Badarī. The Mahābhārata mentions this link several times. Śaṅkara says the Lord remembers because his knowledge is never covered. Rāmānuja stresses that the Lord’s births are real, not illusory. Most of us forget our past lives; the Gītā says this forgetting is part of being bound.",
 "We are part of a longer story than we can remember. That can make us humbler and more patient with ourselves.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Ajo’pi sann avyayātmā</i>, “though unborn and of imperishable self”. <i>Bhūtānām īśvaro’pi san</i>, “though Lord of beings”. <i>Prakṛtiṃ svām adhiṣṭhāya</i>, “governing my own nature”. <i>Sambhavāmy ātma-māyayā</i>, “I come into being by my own power”.",
 "The verse holds two truths together: the Lord is unborn, yet takes birth. Commentators resolve the paradox in different ways. Śaṅkara: the birth is an appearance through the Lord’s <i>māyā</i>, his power, without any change in his real nature. Rāmānuja: the Lord takes a real, divine body made of pure sattva (<i>śuddha-sattva</i>), by his own will; <i>māyā</i> here means his wonderful knowledge and will. Madhva: the Lord’s body is not material at all; it is his own form of knowledge and bliss. All three agree that the avatāra is not bound by karma.",
 "The Divine is not distant. In this view it chooses to come close, out of care for the world.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Yadā yadā hi dharmasya</i>, “whenever of dharma”; <i>glānir bhavati bhārata</i>, “there is decline, Bhārata”; <i>abhyutthānam adharmasya</i>, “and rise of adharma”; <i>tadātmānaṃ sṛjāmy aham</i>, “then I send forth myself”.",
 "This verse and the next are perhaps the most widely known in Hinduism, often printed together and chanted. <i>Glāni</i> means fading, weakening, as a plant wilts. Commentators note that the Lord does not wait for dharma to vanish entirely; he comes when it declines. The Purāṇas list many avatāras; the classic ten (Daśāvatāra) include Matsya, Kūrma, Varāha, Narasiṃha, Vāmana, Paraśurāma, Rāma, Balarāma or Buddha (lists differ), Kṛṣṇa and Kalki. The Bhāgavata Purāṇa (1.3) lists twenty-two. Some teachers also read the verse inwardly: whenever our inner dharma weakens, the Divine rises within as conscience.",
 "When things seem to be falling apart, remember: the story does not end there. Help comes, often through ordinary people.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Paritrāṇāya sādhūnām</i>, “for the protection of the good”; <i>vināśāya ca duṣkṛtām</i>, “for the destruction of evil-doers”; <i>dharma-saṃsthāpanārthāya</i>, “for firmly establishing dharma”; <i>sambhavāmi yuge yuge</i>, “I come into being age after age”.",
 "Commentators often ask: does the all-powerful Lord need to take birth to destroy the wicked? He could do it by will alone. Rāmānuja’s answer is famous: the main reason is <i>paritrāṇa</i>, to be near his devotees, who long to see him. Destroying evil is secondary. Madhusūdana says the Lord comes to show the path by example. Śaṅkara says he comes to protect the Vedic dharma that guides people to freedom. A <i>yuga</i> is an age; the four yugas are Satya, Tretā, Dvāpara and Kali. Kṛṣṇa came at the end of the Dvāpara.",
 "Protecting good people is the first purpose here. Defending the vulnerable is part of that divine work, whoever does it.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Janma karma ca me divyam</i>, “my divine birth and deeds”. <i>Evaṃ yo vetti tattvataḥ</i>, “one who knows thus in truth”. <i>Tyaktvā dehaṃ punar janma naiti</i>, “leaving the body, is not reborn”. <i>Mām eti so’rjuna</i>, “he comes to me, Arjuna”.",
 "<i>Divya</i>, divine, marks the difference from ordinary births and deeds: they are free, pure, and for the world’s good. Commentators find a surprising promise here. Simply knowing the avatāra truly brings liberation. Why? Rāmānuja says such knowledge produces love, and love leads to the Lord. Śaṅkara says knowing the Lord as unborn and actionless is knowing the Self. This verse is the basis for the great love of the <i>līlā</i> stories in bhakti traditions. Hearing and singing of Kṛṣṇa’s childhood, Rāma’s life and Narasiṃha’s protection become a path in themselves.",
 "Stories of the Divine are not just tales. Hearing them with understanding can change the heart.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Vīta-rāga-bhaya-krodhāḥ</i>, “free of passion, fear and anger”. <i>Man-mayā mām upāśritāḥ</i>, “absorbed in me, taking refuge in me”. <i>Bahavo jñāna-tapasā pūtāḥ</i>, “many, purified by the austerity of knowledge”. <i>Mad-bhāvam āgatāḥ</i>, “have reached my state”.",
 "This verse links back to 2.56 (free of passion, fear and anger) and forward to the devotion of chapters 9 and 12. Commentators note <i>jñāna-tapas</i>, “the austerity of knowledge”: knowledge itself is the greatest austerity, because it burns away delusion. <i>Mad-bhāva</i>, “my state” or “my being”, is read by Śaṅkara as oneness with the Lord, by Rāmānuja as a nature like the Lord’s, free and pure, and by Madhva as nearness to him. The word <i>bahavaḥ</i>, “many”, is encouraging: the path is well travelled.",
 "Many before you have overcome fear, anger and craving. Their lives are proof that it can be done.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Ye yathā māṃ prapadyante</i>, “as people approach me”. <i>Tāṃs tathaiva bhajāmy aham</i>, “so I receive them”. <i>Mama vartmānuvartante</i>, “they follow my path”. <i>Manuṣyāḥ pārtha sarvaśaḥ</i>, “humans, in every way, Pārtha”.",
 "This verse is often quoted as the Gītā’s statement of religious openness. Ramakrishna and Vivekananda drew on it, and on the Ṛg Vedic line <i>ekaṃ sad viprā bahudhā vadanti</i>, “truth is one, the wise call it by many names” (1.164.46). Commentators read it in different ways. Śaṅkara: the Lord gives each the fruit they seek; those seeking liberation get liberation. Rāmānuja: the Lord responds to each devotee in the form and manner they love. Some traditional teachers read it more narrowly: all paths are under the Lord’s rule, but not all reach the same goal. The verse keeps both readings open.",
 "Respect the sincere seeking of others, even when their path looks different from yours.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Kāṅkṣantaḥ karmaṇāṃ siddhiṃ</i>, “desiring success in actions”. <i>Yajanta iha devatāḥ</i>, “they worship the deities here”. <i>Kṣipraṃ hi mānuṣe loke</i>, “quickly in the human world”. <i>Siddhir bhavati karmajā</i>, “success born of action comes”.",
 "Commentators note that the Gītā does not forbid praying for success. It observes that such prayers work in their own way, because the world of action brings results quickly. But the results are limited and pass. Chapter 7 (7.20–23) explains that those who worship other deities for wishes get them, but such results are finite; those who worship the Lord reach him. Chapter 9 (9.23) adds that even those who worship other gods are, in truth, worshipping the Lord, though not in the right way. The Gītā is generous but clear about higher and lower aims.",
 "It is natural to ask for help. Let your prayers also include something that lasts beyond this year’s success.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Cātur-varṇyaṃ mayā sṛṣṭaṃ</i>, “the four orders were created by me”. <i>Guṇa-karma-vibhāgaśaḥ</i>, “according to the division of quality and work”. <i>Tasya kartāram api māṃ</i>, “though I am its maker”. <i>Viddhy akartāram avyayam</i>, “know me as the non-doer, changeless”.",
 "The four varṇas are Brāhmaṇa (teachers and priests), Kṣatriya (rulers and protectors), Vaiśya (traders and farmers) and Śūdra (workers and servers). Chapter 18 (18.41–44) describes each by its natural qualities. The meaning of this verse is one of the most argued questions in Hindu thought. Traditional commentators, including Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja, generally took varṇa as fixed by birth, shaped by past karma. Many modern teachers, including Vivekananda, Aurobindo and Gandhi in different ways, emphasised guṇa and karma, quality and work, and rejected birth-based discrimination. Dr. B. R. Ambedkar was sharply critical of the varṇa system and of this verse. We present these views without taking sides.",
 "Whatever one’s view of the old system, the verse invites us to honour each person’s nature and gifts, and the work that fits them.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Na māṃ karmāṇi limpanti</i>, “actions do not stain me”. <i>Na me karma-phale spṛhā</i>, “I have no longing for the fruit of action”. <i>Iti māṃ yo’bhijānāti</i>, “one who knows me thus”. <i>Karmabhir na sa badhyate</i>, “is not bound by actions”.",
 "Commentators explain why the Lord is not stained: he acts with no ego and no craving. <i>Limpanti</i>, “smear” or “stain”, comes from <i>lip</i>, to anoint. Karma is pictured like paint that sticks only where there is the glue of desire. Śaṅkara says knowing the Lord as actionless is knowing the Self as actionless, and that frees. Rāmānuja says knowing how the Lord acts teaches us to act the same way. The image of the lotus leaf (5.10) completes the idea: the leaf sits in the water and stays dry.",
 "Act without the glue of craving, and the stains of action will not stick.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Evaṃ jñātvā kṛtaṃ karma</i>, “knowing thus, action was done”. <i>Pūrvair api mumukṣubhiḥ</i>, “by the ancient seekers of freedom too”. <i>Kuru karmaiva tasmāt tvam</i>, “so you too do action”. <i>Pūrvaiḥ pūrvataraṃ kṛtam</i>, “as done by the ancients of old”.",
 "<i>Mumukṣu</i>, one longing for freedom, is a technical term in Vedānta. Śaṅkara’s Vivekacūḍāmaṇi lists <i>mumukṣutva</i>, the longing for liberation, as one of the four qualifications of a student. Commentators note that even such seekers kept acting. Śaṅkara says they acted to purify the mind; once pure, they turned to knowledge. Rāmānuja says action itself was their means of liberation. The repeated <i>pūrvaiḥ pūrvataram</i>, “by the ancients, even earlier”, stresses that this is the old way, not a new invention.",
 "If you long for freedom, keep working. The way out is through, not around.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Kiṃ karma kim akarmeti</i>, “what is action? what is inaction?”. <i>Kavayo’py atra mohitāḥ</i>, “even poets (sages) are confused here”. <i>Tat te karma pravakṣyāmi</i>, “I will tell you that action”. <i>Yaj jñātvā mokṣyase’śubhāt</i>, “knowing which you will be freed from ill”.",
 "<i>Kavi</i> means a poet but also a seer, a wise one. Commentators praise the humility of admitting that even the wise struggle with this question. The next verses (4.17–23) give a careful analysis, and many consider them among the most philosophical in the Gītā. Śaṅkara’s commentary on these verses is long and detailed. He uses them to argue that the Self is actionless and the knower is free of action even while the body acts. Rāmānuja reads them as teaching that action done with knowledge of the self is really a form of knowledge.",
 "Simple-sounding questions can be the deepest. Don’t rush to an answer; let the question open.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Karmaṇo hy api boddhavyaṃ</i>, “action must be understood”; <i>boddhavyaṃ ca vikarmaṇaḥ</i>, “and wrong action”; <i>akarmaṇaś ca boddhavyaṃ</i>, “and inaction”. <i>Gahanā karmaṇo gatiḥ</i>, “deep is the way of action”.",
 "<i>Vikarma</i> is read in different ways. Śaṅkara takes it as forbidden action. Others read it as special or varied action, or as action that is “against the grain”. <i>Gahanā karmaṇo gatiḥ</i> is a common saying in many Indian languages, used when things turn out in unexpected ways. Commentators explain that action is deep because the same outer act can bind or free, be right or wrong, depending on intention, knowledge and circumstance. A surgeon’s knife and a robber’s knife both cut; the act is not the whole story.",
 "Judge actions, including your own, by intention and context, not just by how they look.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Karmaṇy akarma yaḥ paśyed</i>, “who sees inaction in action”; <i>akarmaṇi ca karma yaḥ</i>, “and action in inaction”; <i>sa buddhimān manuṣyeṣu</i>, “he is wise among people”; <i>sa yuktaḥ kṛtsna-karma-kṛt</i>, “he is a yogi, doing all work”.",
 "This is one of the Gītā’s great riddles. Śaṅkara’s reading: the Self never acts; the body, mind and senses act. One who sees “inaction” (the actionless Self) in all action, and sees that even sitting idle the body and mind are “acting”, is wise. Rāmānuja’s reading: action done with knowledge of the self is really a form of knowledge (“inaction”); and knowledge itself is a kind of action. Many modern readers take a practical view: a selfless worker is “not doing” in the binding sense, while an idle person full of desire is “doing” a great deal.",
 "Look beneath the surface of busyness and stillness. True rest can happen while working; restless craving can happen while sitting still.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Yasya sarve samārambhāḥ</i>, “whose every undertaking”. <i>Kāma-saṅkalpa-varjitāḥ</i>, “free of desire and its schemes”. <i>Jñānāgni-dagdha-karmāṇam</i>, “whose actions are burnt in the fire of knowledge”. <i>Tam āhuḥ paṇḍitaṃ budhāḥ</i>, “the wise call him wise”.",
 "<i>Saṅkalpa</i> is the mind’s planning and wishing, the inner drive behind desire. Commentators note the image of the burnt seed: a roasted seed looks like a seed but cannot sprout. Actions done without desire, in the light of knowledge, look like ordinary actions but do not produce future karma. Śaṅkara’s teacher’s teacher, Gauḍapāda, and later Advaitins use this image often. The verse also redefines <i>paṇḍita</i>, the learned one, as one whose actions are free, not one who knows many books. The fire of knowledge returns in 4.37.",
 "Plan, yes; scheme, no. Act from clarity rather than from hidden wants, and your actions grow lighter.")
DEEP[20]=("<i>Tyaktvā karma-phalāsaṅgaṃ</i>, “giving up attachment to the fruit of action”. <i>Nitya-tṛpto nirāśrayaḥ</i>, “ever content, depending on nothing”. <i>Karmaṇy abhipravṛtto’pi</i>, “though fully engaged in action”. <i>Naiva kiñcit karoti saḥ</i>, “he does nothing at all”.",
 "This verse describes the inner state of the free worker. <i>Nitya-tṛpta</i>, ever satisfied; <i>nirāśraya</i>, without support, meaning not leaning on anything outside for happiness or security. Commentators note the paradox at the end: fully engaged, yet doing nothing. Śaṅkara says this person, if he cannot renounce outwardly, still acts without any sense of doership. Rāmānuja says such action is really the practice of knowledge. Madhva says it is action done entirely for God. The image of a mother caring for a child without counting her efforts is sometimes used.",
 "Work done from contentment, not need, feels very different. Try one task today from that place.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Nirāśīr yata-cittātmā</i>, “without hopes, with mind and self controlled”. <i>Tyakta-sarva-parigrahaḥ</i>, “having given up all possessions”. <i>Śārīraṃ kevalaṃ karma kurvan</i>, “doing only bodily action”. <i>Nāpnoti kilbiṣam</i>, “incurs no sin”.",
 "<i>Parigraha</i>, possessiveness, is the opposite of <i>aparigraha</i>, non-possession, one of Patañjali’s five <i>yamas</i> (Yoga Sūtra 2.30). Commentators read “only bodily action” in two ways. Śaṅkara: the renunciant does only what is needed to keep the body alive, like begging for food. Rāmānuja: all actions are done only by the body, without the self’s attachment. Gandhi made <i>aparigraha</i> one of his ashram vows, trying to own as little as possible. The verse invites everyone to examine how much they hold and why.",
 "Possessions are not wrong, but possessiveness is heavy. Ask what you could hold more lightly.")
